## The Clarity Floor

This named concept explains exactly why asscher cuts are different from brilliant cuts when it comes to clarity — and why the industry standard for this shape is VS1, not VS2.

Every diamond has inclusions — tiny natural features inside the rough that the cutter could not remove without sacrificing the stone's proportions. In a brilliant cut, the 58 facets scatter light so aggressively that inclusions get lost in the visual chaos. A VS2 round brilliant looks perfectly eye-clean to most people.

Asscher cuts have 25–50 facets running in long parallel lines. Unlike brilliant-cut facets that scatter light in every direction, these step facets reflect it back. They do not hide inclusions — they amplify them.

Imagine a hall of mirrors — if you put a mark on one mirror, every mirror in the room shows it. Asscher's step facets work the same way. An inclusion near the table reflects in the facet above it, then in the one above that — you see one feather, but you perceive it as far more prominent than it actually is.

This is The Clarity Floor: **VS1 minimum for any asscher cut diamond.**

Some buyers find VS2 asschers that are genuinely eye-clean — it happens. But it requires inspecting the specific stone, not just reading the GIA report. The report tells you inclusions exist; the video tells you where they sit relative to the step facets.

For the [1ct E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the [1ct F-VS1 at $2,521](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29365844?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), The Clarity Floor is already met. You are buying safely.

The practical difference between VS2 and VS1 in price at 1ct is typically $100–$200. That is not a meaningful saving against the risk of an asscher that looks included under normal viewing conditions. The Clarity Floor is the single most important rule for this shape — and it costs very little to follow it.

For lab asscher cuts, the floor goes higher at larger sizes. The [1.52ct D-IF lab at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) shows you why lab buyers often jump to D-IF in asscher — the step facets make anything below VVS2 a risk at 1.5ct+. In lab diamonds, D-IF is achievable at a fraction of the natural equivalent, so buyers take it.

[![Asscher cut diamond ring with baguette Art Deco channel setting in white gold architectural style](/images/asscher-cut-diamond-ring-baguette-channel-white-gold-art-deco.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/asscher-cut-diamond-rings?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

